22:53:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very natural assumption, and it’s what classical thermodynamics says *should* happen—in a perfectly isolated system left alone for long enough, everything eventually reaches the same temperature. But a real room is never that isolated system. Let me clarify exactly why things don’t just settle to a single “room temperature.” ### The core idea: steady state vs. true equilibrium Imagine you put a pot of water on a gas stove, set the flame to low, and wait. After a while, the water doesn’t keep getting hotter forever—it stabilises at some temperature (say 60 °C) and stays there. Even though the air in the kitchen might be 20 °C, the water will *not* eventually cool down to 20 °C as long as the flame remains on. Why? Because heat is constantly flowing *into* the water from the flame, and *out of* the water into the cooler room. The water reaches a **steady state** where energy in = energy out, but its temperature is permanently offset from the air temperature. A real room works the same way: - There are **continuous heat inputs**: radiators, electronics, sunlight, even your own body (≈100 W). - There are **continuous heat leaks**: cold windows, exterior walls, air drafts, ventilation. So objects don’t drift towards a single magic number; they each find their own steady-state temperature where the heat flowing into them equals the heat flowing out. This temperature depends on their location and properties, and it will not be identical to the air temperature measured at the thermostat. ### Specific reasons objects don’t converge to one temperature 1. **Persistent local heat sources/sinks** A laptop that’s been running for hours doesn’t “become room temperature” because it’s constantly generating heat. It reaches a steady temperature maybe 15–20 °C above the air. A glass of ice water never reaches room temperature until all the ice is melted; it stays at ~0 °C as long as ice remains, because melting absorbs heat at a constant rate. 2. **Radiation coupling to different-temperature surfaces** Even if the air is uniformly 21 °C, a wall with a cold window “seen” by an object will cause a net radiation loss. That object can stabilise 1–2 °C below the air, while another object facing a warm radiator stabilises above it. Both are in steady state but at different temperatures, indefinitely. 3. **Thermal mass and air temperature swings** Real room air temperature is not steady—it cycles up and down as heating switches on/off, the sun moves, etc. Heavy objects (concrete floor, water tank) have a long thermal time constant (many hours). If the air warms up for two hours, the slab might only gain a fraction of a degree, never “catching up” before the air cools again. So at any moment, massive items are often lagging behind the air temperature. 4. **Stratification** Warm air rises, so the ceiling is warmer than the floor. A book on a high shelf sits in warmer air than a shoe on the floor; they’ll never simultaneously be at one “room temperature” because they’re immersed in different air layers. So in short: **objects don’t simply become room temperature because the room is not an isolated box that drifts towards equilibrium. It’s a system with persistent energy flows, so everything ends up in a personal steady state—not a universal one.** If you were to seal the room perfectly, turn off all appliances, remove all people, and wait many hours (maybe a day or more), *then* you would find everything eventually converging to a single, uniform temperature. That’s the equilibrium you intuitively expect. But any real, occupied room never gets there.
